  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Uganda and in Mbale and Kumi district</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Youth Advocacy for Decision-Making/Action (YADA) is a youth-led initiative aimed at empowering marginalized youth leaders in Mbale and Kumi district to actively engage in budget advocacy, influence policy, and ensure youth priorities are integrated into decision-making processes. Through capacity-building training, strategic district engagements, and participation in key committee meetings, YADA strengthens the role of young people in advocating for their needs, particularly in Adolescent and Youth Sexual and ReproductiveHealth (AYSRH) and resource allocation. By 2026, the project will enhance youth representation, ensuring inclusive policies, improved resource distribution, and sustainable development for young people.

This project aims to strengthen youth leadership, representation, and advocacy to amplify the voices of young people, particularly adolescent girls and young women, in decision-making processes. It seeks to build the capacity of youth leaders, district teams, and stakeholders in budget advocacy and policy engagement whileequipping HUMC youth representatives to influence resource allocation and address AYSRH needs. Through barazas and district meetings, youth will present key issues and advocate for inclusive policies. The intended outcomes include enhanced youth participation, increased resource allocation for youth-friendly services, and improved governance that addresses youth needs.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="1" type="10">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">European Union</narrative>
  </participating-org>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MNYPA and MHAGYW have collaborated to create a stronger, more unified movement advocating for the rights and well-being of young people, particularly those affected by HIV and gender-based inequalities. Together, they work to empower youth, promote health, and drive policy change across Bugisu and Teso sub region.

MNYPA was founded to address the unique challenges faced by young people living with and affected by HIV. Through advocacy, education, and empowerment, it focuses on SRHR, HIV prevention, stigma reduction, gender-based violence (GBV), ICT, and socioeconomic empowerment. Over the years, MNYPA has strengthened youth participation in decision-making and collaborated with partners to improve access to essential services.

MHAGYW, established in 2019, is committed to supporting rural adolescent girls and young women (AGYW) in districts such as Ngora, Kumi, and Soroti by advocating for health, gender equality, and justice. It combats human rights violations like stigma and discrimination while improving access to HIV, TB, SRHR, and GBV services. Through mentorship, leadership training, and economic empowerment programs, MHAGYW equips AGYW with the skills and confidence to advocate for their rights.

Together, MNYPA and MHAGYW envision a world where young people, especially AGYW and those living with HIV, can access the resources and opportunities needed for a healthy and empowered future. They are guided by shared values of integrity, transparency, teamwork, accountability, and innovation. Their combined efforts focus on strengthening institutional capacity, reducing vulnerability to HIV, promoting gender equality, and influencing policies that impact young people&#x2019;s lives.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Mbale Network of Young People Livin</narrative>
